# AI Feature Contract

- All API-owned LLM calls must resolve providers through `resolveOrgAI(tenantId, feature)` or `resolveAIProvider`. Do not instantiate provider SDKs directly in app code. The collector vendor-research path is the current documented exception — TODO: remove the exception once that path is refactored to use `resolveOrgAI`.
- Every new user-facing AI accelerator must be advisory: write proposals, drafts, or pending rows only. A human action must approve any customer-record mutation.
- For Enterprise AI surfaces, gate every entry point with `assertEnterpriseLicense("ai")`; local dev may use `TRUSTALO_LICENSE_DEV_BYPASS=1`, but production must fail closed.
- Audit every AI invocation or decision with `feature`, `provider`, `model`, `providerSource` where available, `outcome`, and enough non-secret context for review. Use the existing `audit(req, ...)` helper for API routes.
- Rate-limit AI endpoints per tenant and feature in addition to the global limiter when the route can trigger model work or background collection.
- Scrub PII before sending user-supplied free text to an external provider when the feature handles chat, pasted context, questionnaires, or uploaded document content.
- Parse model output through Zod schemas or existing validated helpers. Do not trust raw `JSON.parse` results without schema validation and recovery/error handling.
- Keep `packages/ai/src/types.ts`, Prisma `AIFeature`, `docs/ai-features.md`, and the UI feature labels/settings in sync when adding or renaming a feature key.
- Provider and model selection must remain operator/org configurable. Do not hardcode a provider or model in a product feature except for explicit test probes or documented collector-only paths.
- Update `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` only if the AI checklist itself changes; otherwise satisfy its AI feature checklist in the PR.
